# Python bytecode and interpreter caches
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# Virtual environments and local Python installations
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/
__pypackages__/

# Test, type-checking, linting, and coverage artifacts
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/

# The standalone subproject's lockfile, which this repository does not use.
# `subprojects/docket/` carries its own pyproject.toml, so `uv` run from inside
# it resolves and writes one - but the root pyproject.toml already declares
# those tests in `testpaths`, so they belong to the root environment and the
# root `uv.lock`. Anchored rather than a bare `uv.lock`, which would also match
# the tracked root lockfile (`PL-8PT6`).
/subprojects/docket/uv.lock

# Packaging and build output
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
wheels/

# Generated output, and the one place it goes. A session that renders the
# interface writes a PNG somewhere, and before this there was no somewhere:
# `docs/worker.md` told every worker to grab `dashboard.png` into the working
# directory, which is the repository root, untracked and unignored. One
# directory rather than a pattern like `*.png`, which would swallow a real
# asset the day this project carries one (`PL-CNJ1`).
#
# `docs/worker.md` (the screenshot a session takes to look at the interface)
# is what names it for a reader; this is what keeps it out of a commit.
# Nothing tracked lives here, so it is safe to delete at any time.
/out/

# Flet runtime and local application state
/.flet/
/storage/
/uploads/

# Local working directories at the repository root, which exist in the project
# owner's checkout and in no commit: `git log --all -- oas-mirror output
# outputs` is empty for all three. Nothing ignored them either, so each sat in
# `git status` as untracked - the moment `PL-1YDK` describes as the one where a
# stray file gets swept into someone else's diff. `PL-DLR3` carries the finding.
#
# Anchored with a leading `/`, like the runtime directories above: `output` and
# `outputs` are ordinary enough words that an unanchored rule would also hide a
# real `output/` inside a package.
#
# `/oas-mirror/` is a clone rather than scratch, and ignoring it does more than
# tidy the listing. `git add` on a directory that contains its own `.git`
# records a gitlink - mode 160000, pointing at a commit this repository has no
# `.gitmodules` entry for - so a clone of this repository gets a reference it
# cannot resolve rather than the files. Git prints a warning, which is easy to
# miss inside a `git add -A`. `PL-548V` covers the rule that tells sessions to
# make such a clone without naming a destination.
/oas-mirror/
/output/
/outputs/

# Local environment variables and secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Logs and temporary files
*.log
*.tmp
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# macOS metadata
.DS_Store
._*

# Editor state, and the two halves are not the same. `.idea/` is entirely
# local. `.vscode/` is not: `settings.json` is tracked project configuration -
# the interpreter path, pytest, and ruff as the format-on-save formatter - so
# the directory's *contents* are excluded and the shared files re-admitted by
# name. Form taken from `github/gitignore`'s
# `Global/VisualStudioCode.gitignore`, minus its `!*.code-workspace`, which
# negates an exclude this file does not have and so would read as if workspace
# files were ignored somewhere (`PL-RFGY`).
#
# `.vscode/*` rather than `.vscode/`, and the difference is the whole bug this
# replaced: git does not descend into an excluded *directory*, so every
# negation under a bare `.vscode/` is dead and `extensions.json` stayed
# unaddable while `settings.json` sat tracked beside it.
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/tasks.json
!.vscode/launch.json
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/*.code-snippets

# Claude Code personal overrides (project settings are committed)
.claude/settings.local.json
